### 2. What You Can Deduct (Above-the-Line vs. Business Deductions)
These deductions lower your Adjusted Gross Income (AGI) before you even decide whether to itemize or take the standard deduction:
*   **Student Loan Interest:** You paid $2,700, but the IRS caps the student loan interest deduction at **$2,500** per year. You can take this $2,500 as an "above-the-line" deduction on Schedule 1.
*   **Home Office Deduction:** Because you have exclusive use of your home office for your consulting work, you can deduct this against your $8,900 in 1099 income on Schedule C. 
    *   *Simplified Method:* 165 sq ft × $5 = $825 deduction.
    *   *Actual Expenses Method:* 165 sq ft / 690 sq ft = ~23.9% of your home. Your annual rent is $24,600 ($2,050 × 12). 23.9% of $24,600 is **$5,879.40**. You can also deduct 23.9% of your utilities and renter's insurance. The actual expenses method will save you much more money here.
*   **Work Expenses ($650):** 
