Here is a breakdown of how your situation is generally handled for the 2024 tax year:

### 1. Income Reporting Breakdown
*   **W-2 Wages ($79,400):** Reported on Form 1040, Line 1.
*   **Freelance Income ($8,900):** Reported on **Schedule C** (Profit or Loss from Business). You will also need to calculate self-employment tax on this income using **Schedule SE**.
*   **Interest ($920) & Dividends ($480):** Reported directly on Form 1040 (Lines 2b and 3b). Because the total is under $1,500, you are not strictly required to file Schedule B, though tax software often generates it anyway.

### 2. Above-the-Line Deductions
*   **Student Loan Interest ($4,800):** You can deduct up to **$2,500** of student loan interest as an "adjustment to income" (above-the-line deduction) on **Schedule 1**. This means you get this deduction even if you take the standard deduction. The remaining $2,300 is not deductible. 

### 3. Standard vs. Itemized Deductions
*   **Charitable Contribution ($800):** This is an itemized deduction (Schedule A).
*   **The Verdict:** For 2024, the Standard Deduction for Married Filing Jointly is **$29,200**. Because your itemized deductions ($800) are significantly lower than $29,200, you should **take the Standard Deduction**. It will save you much more money and time.

### 4. Home Office & Work Supplies Treatment
*W-2 employees can no longer deduct unreimbursed employee expenses or home offices. However, because you have freelance income, you can deduct these expenses against your freelance business!*

*   **Work Supplies ($780):** Fully deductible as a business expense on your **Schedule C**.
*   **Home Office Deduction:** You can deduct the portion of your home used exclusively for your freelance graphic design business. Your office is 165 sq ft out of 690 sq ft, which is roughly **23.9%** of your home. You have two ways to calculate this:
    *   **Simplified Method:** 165 sq ft × $5/sq ft = **$825 deduction**. (Very easy, no receipts required).
    *   **Actual Expenses Method:** 23.9% of your total rent ($2,100 × 12 months = $25,200/year). 23.9% of $25,200 is **$6,022**. You can also add 23.9% of your utilities and renter's insurance. This method requires **Form 8829** but will give you a significantly larger deduction.

### 5. Necessary Forms to Use
When filing, you will need the following forms:
*   **Form 1040:** Your main tax return.
*   **Schedule 1:** To report your business income and claim your $2,500 student loan interest deduction.
*   **Schedule C:** To report your $8,900 freelance income and deduct your $780 in supplies.
*   **Form 8829:** To calculate and claim your home office deduction (if you choose the Actual Expenses method).
*   **Schedule SE:** To calculate self-employment tax on your net freelance profit. 

*Note: Be sure to keep your Northwest Harvest receipt and your work supply receipts with your tax records for at least 3 years, even if you take the standard deduction.*
